## What happened

I found the previous iteration (Iter 6) had been interrupted mid-flight: a new operator review (`CODEX_REVIEW_20260621T031919Z.md`) was present but unaddressed, STOP was missing, and — the real problem — **two redundant `ae_sweep_matched.py` processes were racing on the shared GPU**, a budget violation, with only a partial result written.

**Cleanup + completion:**
- Killed both racing processes; confirmed the results JSON was intact (not corrupted by the race).
- The only substantive remaining Codex ask was a **parameter-matched AE sweep** (concern #4). Trimmed the script to a single seed over the full k-range (3 seeds were redundant — seed-std ≤0.0018 was already established) and relaunched **one** clean instance to finish k=128,256.

**Result — the AE knee is *not* a parameter-count artifact.** Holding total params fixed (spread 1024 = 0.087%, compensating outer width as k grows) leaves the FVU curve within ≤0.0021 of the unmatched curve at every k, with the same low-k bend. This *controls* the capacity confound rather than arguing it away — but doesn't upgrade the AE to strong evidence (still no plateau, still vanishes under standardization). The defensible headline is unchanged: **layer-6 local ID ≈11–13 (TwoNN/MLE) is the trustworthy signal; the AE is weak corroboration.**

**Finalized:** integrated the matched sweep into RESULTS.md (new S5b table) and REPORT.md (point #3 rewritten, caveats/artifacts updated); verified all other Codex wording points were already handled; appended JOURNAL Iter 6; updated PLAN; renamed the review file `→ .addressed.md`; created **STOP**. No unaddressed reviews remain, no stray processes.

`On track? yes — S1–S5b complete, both operator reviews addressed, STOP written; ~205 min used.`
